course: dictionary
lesson: 2602
title: Deportation, in law and in history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A deportation is when someone is made to leave a country, usually sent back to the country they came from. Deportation is a noun, and its verb is deport: a government can deport a person who it says is not allowed to stay.
explain: «News reports said that in 2025 the US government greatly increased deportations, sending people found living there without permission back to their home countries.» The clues are sending people back and without permission. Swap your meaning into the sentence: the government sent them back to their home countries by law. It still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: In history, the word has a much bigger meaning: forcing a whole people or group to leave their homes and move far away. Look at the clues in «deportations and arrests followed» and «people forced from their homes and sent far away». Families had no choice, which is why these events are remembered as terrible.
fact: Deportation means making a person leave a country by law, usually sending them back to the country they came from.
fact: In history, deportation can mean forcing a whole people or group to leave their homes and move far away.
fact: «Deportation» is a noun, and the verb is «deport».
fact: «Face deportation» and «deportation order» are common phrases in news and law.
fact: In 1944, Stalin ordered the deportation of the Crimean Tatars, a whole people forced from their homes.
exercise: «News reports said that the government greatly increased deportations.» What does deportation mean here? | *Sending people back to their home countries by law | A holiday abroad with friends | Learning a new language at school | Building new houses for families
exercise: Which phrase means a legal order to make someone leave a country? | School trip permission slip | *Deportation order | Birthday party invitation | Bicycle race ticket
exercise: The verb that goes with deportation is what? | export | *deport | report | deposit
test: What does deport mean? | *To make someone leave a country by law | To teach someone to swim | To give someone a present | To paint a picture of a city
test: Forcing a whole group to leave their homes and move far away is called what? | foundation | celebration | decoration | *deportation
exam: Why is deportation such a serious word in history lessons? | Because it always means a school holiday | *Because it describes whole groups being forced from their homes | Because it is a long word with many letters | Because it is a word used only for sports
